  "slug": "therapy-brain-scans-d573",
  "summary": "Recent preprints convert molecular trajectories into mechanism-specific quantities that can be compared directly across systems and methods.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is the reduction of molecular trajectories to quantitative readouts tied to a specific physical mechanism. ExaTrack benchmarks a single-particle tracking tool that models diffusive, directed, and confined motion with transition kinetics. PySteMoDA is an open-source Python package with a GUI for automated analysis of constant-velocity steered molecular dynamics, including force-peak identification. The bilayer graphene nanopore study applies molecular dynamics to p53 peptides, reporting that post-translational modifications alter dwell times more than blockade and that a support vector machine distinguishes acetyl and phospho states. What is new is the use of a computed observable as the decision variable: a motion-model parameter, a force-peak descriptor, or a classifiable translocation signature. Maturity and scope differ across these three preprints, so the shared element is an explicit link between computation and a specific mechanism rather than a common methodology.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-01-29"
}
